WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.949
A strong pattern is to accept constrained
file names or resource identifiers instead

2
00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:05.709
of raw paths.

3
00:00:05.789 --> 00:00:08.816
If a workflow only needs a report
identifier, an image key,

4
00:00:08.896 --> 00:00:12.665
or a simple export name, the interface
should ask for that smaller value.

5
00:00:12.745 --> 00:00:16.762
The application can then map it into an
approved root using rules it controls.

6
00:00:16.842 --> 00:00:21.167
Allowlisting is usually clearer than
trying to recognize every dangerous string.

7
00:00:21.247 --> 00:00:23.533
Define the characters, length, extension,

8
00:00:23.613 --> 00:00:26.085
and naming format the workflow actually
needs.

9
00:00:26.165 --> 00:00:29.378
Reject values that are empty, too long,
ambiguous, absolute,

10
00:00:29.458 --> 00:00:32.794
contain unexpected separators, use
platform-reserved names,

11
00:00:32.874 --> 00:00:34.666
or do not fit the supported format.

12
00:00:34.746 --> 00:00:38.886
The specific policy may vary by platform
and product, but it should be explicit.

13
00:00:38.966 --> 00:00:42.797
Metadata-derived names deserve the same
treatment as direct user input.

14
00:00:42.877 --> 00:00:46.955
Uploaded filenames, archive entry names,
document titles, exported labels,

15
00:00:47.035 --> 00:00:50.434
and names read from other files can all
carry external influence.

16
00:00:50.514 --> 00:00:52.429
They may be useful as display labels,

17
00:00:52.509 --> 00:00:56.031
but they should not be trusted as
destinations without validation.

18
00:00:56.111 --> 00:01:00.436
Validation should happen before path
construction and before the file operation.

19
00:01:00.516 --> 00:01:04.780
That ordering matters because later code
should deal with a resource name that has

20
00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.073
already been reduced to the safe shape the
workflow supports.

21
00:01:08.153 --> 00:01:12.169
If path construction accepts arbitrary
strings and then tries to repair them

22
00:01:12.249 --> 00:01:15.092
afterward, the design becomes harder to
reason about.

23
00:01:15.172 --> 00:01:17.890
The goal is not to recognize every
possible bad path.

24
00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:22.110
The goal is to accept only values that are
clearly part of the intended workflow.

25
00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:26.392
That shift makes the implementation
simpler, makes test cases more meaningful,

26
00:01:26.472 --> 00:01:28.820
and gives reviewers a concrete rule to
check.
